--- title Arithmetic beyond the four operations
--- about The whole-number functions and the two names each of the
--- about trigonometric ones carry. What is pinned here is mostly the
--- about boundaries: what these answer at zero, at one, and for the
--- about negatives and wrong types where the obvious guess is wrong.

--- id maths/greatest-common-divisor
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
gcd 12 18
--- result
6

--- id maths/common-divisor-with-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Zero divides into anything, so the answer is the other side
--- note rather than zero or an error.
--- code
gcd 0 5
--- result
5

--- id maths/common-divisor-of-two-zeroes
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
gcd 0 0
--- result
0

--- id maths/common-divisor-ignores-sign
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
gcd -12 18
--- result
6

--- id maths/common-divisor-refuses-a-fraction
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Divisors are a whole-number idea, so a decimal is refused
--- note rather than truncated.
--- code
gcd 12.5 5
--- error script expect-arg

--- id maths/common-divisor-refuses-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
gcd "a" 1
--- error script expect-arg

--- id maths/lowest-common-multiple
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
lcm 4 6
--- result
12

--- id maths/lowest-common-multiple-with-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Zero rather than the other side, which is the opposite of what
--- note GCD does with a zero.
--- code
lcm 0 5
--- result
0

--- id maths/lowest-common-multiple-of-two-zeroes
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
lcm 0 0
--- result
0

--- id maths/the-smallest-prime
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
prime? 2
--- result
#(true)

--- id maths/an-odd-prime
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
prime? 7
--- result
#(true)

--- id maths/one-is-not-prime
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The boundary just below the first prime, and the one people
--- note get wrong.
--- code
prime? 1
--- result
#(false)

--- id maths/zero-is-not-prime
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
prime? 0
--- result
#(false)

--- id maths/a-negative-is-not-prime
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
prime? -7
--- result
#(false)

--- id maths/whole-division-drops-the-remainder
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
integer-divide 7 2
--- result
3

--- id maths/whole-division-of-a-negative-rounds-towards-zero
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Towards zero rather than downwards, so -3 and not -4.
--- code
integer-divide -7 2
--- result
-3

--- id maths/whole-division-by-zero-raises
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
integer-divide 7 0
--- error math zero-divide

--- id maths/radians-to-degrees
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to-degrees 3.14159265358979
--- result
180.0

--- id maths/degrees-to-radians
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
to-radians 180
--- result
3.14159265358979

--- id maths/the-long-name-takes-degrees
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note SINE and SIN are not two names for one function. This one
--- note takes degrees, and the quarter turn is one.
--- code
sine 90
--- result
1.0

--- id maths/the-short-name-takes-radians
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The same number through the other one is the sine of ninety
--- note radians, which is nowhere near. Defining either as an alias of
--- note the other is wrong by a factor of fifty-seven.
--- code
sin 90.0
--- result
0.893996663600558

--- id maths/the-short-name-refuses-a-whole-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note And this is the other half of the difference: the long names
--- note take a whole number and the short ones do not.
--- code
sin 90
--- error script expect-arg

--- id maths/the-long-square-root-takes-a-whole-number
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
square-root 16
--- result
4.0

--- id maths/the-short-square-root-does-not
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note There is no angle here at all, and the split still holds, so
--- note it is about the argument type rather than about radians.
--- code
sqrt 16
--- error script expect-arg

--- id maths/the-short-square-root-takes-a-decimal
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
sqrt 16.0
--- result
4.0

--- id maths/the-angle-of-a-point
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
atan2 1.0 1.0
--- result
0.785398163397448

--- id maths/the-angle-of-a-point-refuses-whole-numbers
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Which is the one place in the maths natives where a whole
--- note number is not accepted where a decimal is wanted.
--- code
atan2 1 1
--- error script expect-arg
